WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.284
Authorization answers a different question
from authentication:

2
00:00:06.364 --> 00:00:09.104
what may this connected user do?

3
00:00:09.184 --> 00:00:14.969
An authenticated connection proves an
identity or client context.

4
00:00:15.049 --> 00:00:20.630
It does not automatically prove that the
caller may use every hub,

5
00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:25.886
invoke every method, read every resource,
join every group,

6
00:00:25.966 --> 00:00:29.011
or receive every targeted message.

7
00:00:29.091 --> 00:00:35.991
SignalR applications can use hub-level
authorization to protect an entire hub.

8
00:00:36.071 --> 00:00:42.059
That is useful when every method on the
hub requires a common baseline,

9
00:00:42.139 --> 00:00:47.923
such as an authenticated account or a
specific application policy.

10
00:00:48.003 --> 00:00:55.208
Method-level authorization is useful when
sensitive actions need stronger checks

11
00:00:55.288 --> 00:01:00.160
than ordinary notifications or user-level
operations.

12
00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:07.242
Policy-based authorization helps express
requirements in maintainable terms.

13
00:01:07.322 --> 00:01:12.903
A policy may depend on roles, claims,
scopes, tenant membership,

14
00:01:12.983 --> 00:01:19.072
subscription state, administrative status,
or other business rules.

15
00:01:19.152 --> 00:01:26.256
For resource-sensitive actions, the hub
often needs to check trusted server-side

16
00:01:26.336 --> 00:01:29.076
data at the time the method runs.

17
00:01:29.156 --> 00:01:33.317
Ownership, tenant boundaries, account
status,

18
00:01:33.397 --> 00:01:40.500
and delegated permissions should not be
accepted only because a client supplied an

19
00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:41.697
identifier.

20
00:01:41.777 --> 00:01:45.633
UI hiding is not server-side
authorization.

21
00:01:45.713 --> 00:01:51.193
A button may be hidden for a user who
should not perform an action,

22
00:01:51.273 --> 00:01:55.637
but the hub method still needs its own
enforcement.

23
00:01:55.717 --> 00:02:01.197
Clients can be outdated, modified,
automated, or simply wrong.

24
00:02:01.277 --> 00:02:08.178
The server is the place where
authorization decisions must be made and recorded.

25
00:02:08.258 --> 00:02:12.824
Groups are also not a complete
authorization system.

26
00:02:12.904 --> 00:02:16.152
They are useful for routing messages,

27
00:02:16.232 --> 00:02:23.538
but group membership must be managed by
trusted server-side decisions and refreshed

28
00:02:23.618 --> 00:02:25.851
when permissions change.

29
00:02:25.931 --> 00:02:27.859
If a user loses access,

30
00:02:27.939 --> 00:02:34.637
active connections and group membership
may need explicit removal, refresh,

31
00:02:34.717 --> 00:02:37.051
or reconnection behavior.

32
00:02:37.131 --> 00:02:43.220
Treat groups as delivery structure, not as
the whole permission model.
